Change: Tell users about the brute-force wait instead of just being slow

Repeated failed logins are still rate-limited, but the login form now explains
the wait. Three things changed:

The first three failed attempts on an account no longer cost anything. People
mistype passwords, and charging for the very first try only teaches them to
distrust the form. Past that the cooldown climbs 5, 10, 20, 40, 80 seconds up to
two minutes, and a successful login clears the counter for that account.

Waiting now counts. The cooldown is the minimum spacing between attempts, so a
user who sits out the wait is let through instead of being charged the full delay
a second time. The attacker's maximum rate is unchanged - one attempt per
cooldown either way.

The web login refuses an attempt during the cooldown before checking the
credentials and shows how long is left, counting down live, with the sign-in
button disabled for exactly that long. Previously the request slept server-side
and the page simply appeared to hang, with nothing to tell the user why or for
how long. Not sleeping also means no web server process is held while an
attacker waits.

Password spraying and subnet-wide guessing are still detected, unchanged, and
there is still no permanent lockout: every cooldown expires by itself, so nobody
can lock a colleague out of their account.
